              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-369",
              ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2}{3} x \\,\\textrm d x.$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{3}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "add45606-39f6-5a61-9535-867d2950d183",
