          {
            ":db/id": 17592186096617,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186096417,
              ":question/id": "d7a77a89-0535-54da-a246-36ee5b4e9e7f",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-3634",
              ":question/problem": "Differentiate $x\\cos x.$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\cos x .$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t (x\\cos x)' &=   u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x)\\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t&= (x)'\\cdot \\cos x + x\\cdot (\\cos x)'\\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t   & = 1 \\cdot \\cos x + x (-\\sin x)\\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t  & =  \\cos x - x \\sin x\t\t\t\t\t\n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186096618,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186096418,
              ":question/id": "6bb0eded-7277-5ab5-8c13-bfc480403b1a",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-3635",
              ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=x^2\\ln x,$ find $f'(x).$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = x^2$ and $v(x)=\\ln x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & ={({x}^{2})}^{\u2032}ln\u2061x+{x}^{2}{(ln\u2061x)}^{\u2032} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot ln\u2061x+{x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =2xln\u2061x+x \\\\  & =x(2ln\u2061x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186096619,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186096419,
              ":question/id": "072ce609-b04d-57b0-9c7d-cbb8765c38fa",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-3636",
              ":question/problem": "If a curve $\\mathcal C$ is given by $y=e^x\\sin x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\dfrac \\pi 2.$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^x$ and $v(x)=\\sin{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = e^x,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot cos\u2061x+sin\u2061x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(cos\u2061x+sin\u2061x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi /2} & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(cos\u2061\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+sin\u2061\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e^{\\pi/2}.$"
            },
